Keep the compiled code across a state load and a reset.

Why: the WASM JIT compiles each SH4 block into its own WebAssembly module, synchronously
(patches/0005 made that deterministic), and a cache reset throws all of them away: the game
then recompiles about 2600 blocks over the next 4-5 s, frames of 100-300 ms among them. The
bar's worker loads a state into a power-on machine on every machine whenever the players
change (retro_reset, then retro_unserialize), and in lockstep each machine's hitch is a wait
on the others, so the game crawled for seconds after every join. The blocks are verified
against the RAM they were compiled from at dispatch (the fork's page-generation gate and
full-block hash, its answer to self-modifying code), so on this backend a cache reset now
only marks every RAM page as written: each block re-verifies once, and only code that really
changed is recompiled. The compiled code bakes the addresses of the RAM, VRAM and sound RAM
in, and the SH4 context block's, and a game reload (retro_reset) freed and allocated them again,
elsewhere once the JIT had allocated in between: they now stay put across a reload, and one that did
move still costs the full reset. Nothing the compiled code does depends on when it was
compiled.

